[2026-08-02T16:58:37.385Z] [INFO]         "content": "# npm Trusted Publishing via GitHub Actions\n\n## Requirements\n\nAccording to the documentation, you need:\n\n- **npm CLI version 11.5.1 or later** and **Node version 22.14.0 or higher**\n- The critical workflow permission: **`id-token: [REDACTED] to allow GitHub Actions to generate OIDC tokens\n- A configured trusted publisher on npmjs.com matching your repository, workflow filename (with `.yml`/`.yaml` extension), and organization/username\n\n## Interference with Existing authToken\n\nThe documentation does not explicitly address whether an existing `_authToken` in `.npmrc` interferes with trusted publishing. However, it notes that \"The npm CLI automatically detects OIDC environments and uses them for authentication before falling back to traditional tokens,\" suggesting OIDC takes precedence.\n\n## Error When Trusted Publisher Not Configured\n\nWhen publishing without proper trusted publisher setup, you receive an \"**Unable to authenticate**\" (ENEEDAUTH) error. The documentation stresses: \"npm does not verify your trusted publisher configuration when you save it. Double-check that your repository, workflow filename, and other details are correct, as errors will only appear when you attempt to publish.\"\n\n## Creating New Packages\n\nThe documentation does not address whether trusted publishing can create brand new packages. It focuses exclusively on publishing existing packages already registered on npmjs.com."
