[2026-08-02T17:08:54.821Z] [INFO]         "content": "Perhaps it's specific to pnpm workspaces.\n\nBut I [tried](https://github.com/developmentseed/deck.gl-raster/actions/runs/25821422344) [several](https://github.com/developmentseed/deck.gl-raster/actions/runs/25822153872) [times](https://github.com/developmentseed/deck.gl-raster/actions/runs/25822517936) to publish, all using a configuration that had worked just fine two weeks prior. And I didn't change anything on the NPM side. The only thing that [worked](https://github.com/developmentseed/deck.gl-raster/actions/runs/25823352662) for me was removing the dummy token.\n\n> For error code details, please refer to the [npm OIDC API documentation](https://api-docs.npmjs.com/#tag/OIDC).\n\nI don't see any information that would explain the error.\n--\nauthor:\tchiranjib-swain\nassociation:\tcontributor\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nHi everyone, we opened PR #1558 to remove the dummy/default `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN` export. With this change, the action will only export an auth token when the workflow explicitly provides one, rather than implicitly setting a token when users haven’t provided it.\n\nEven though OIDC publishing doesn’t rely on `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN`,  as highlighted in prior discussions , the dummy token can still cause confusion and unexpected behavior. In particular, it may end up influencing the generated `.npmrc` by injecting a non-functional token value into the environment. To keep the environment clean and avoid confusion, we proposed removing the dummy token export.\nThanks in advance!\n--\nauthor:\tchadlwilson\nassociation:\tnone\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\n> Hi everyone, we opened PR [#1558](https://github.com/actions/setup-node/pull/1558) to remove the dummy/default `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN` export. With this change, the action will only export an auth token when the workflow explicitly provides one, rather than implicitly setting a token when users haven’t provided it.\n\nThis sounds good @chiranjib-swain - I wish you the best of luck with the change and rollout!\n\n--\nauthor:\tgowridurgad\nassociation:\tcontributor\nedited:\ttrue\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nHi everyone [PR #1558 ](https://github.com/actions/setup-node/pull/1558)has now been merged. To use this change immediately, you can use actions/setup-node@main in your workflows for now, and it will be reflected in the upcoming release. We’ll close this issue, but feel free to reach out if you have more questions or need help.\n--\nauthor:\thnrobert\nassociation:\tnone\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nWhen executing `npm publish` on GitHub Action, using `node-version: \"20\"` would result in a failure like this:\n\n```bash\nnpm notice publish Signed provenance statement with source and build information from GitHub Actions\nnpm notice publish Provenance statement published to transparency log: https://search.sigstore.dev/?logIndex=1751525321\nnpm error code E404\nnpm error 404 Not Found - PUT https://registry.npmjs.org/@markuxt%2fmarkuxt - Not found\nnpm error 404\nnpm error 404  '@markuxt/markuxt@0.1.13' is not in this registry.\nnpm error 404\nnpm error 404 Note that you can also install from a\nnpm error 404 tarball, folder, http url, or git url.\n```\n\n**Simpliy switch to node version `24` when publishing would solve the issue...**\n\nThat may implicitly reflect the `NPM_AUTH_TOKEN` issue, but no explicit `NPM_AUTH_TOKEN` unsetting or resetting is required to have this issue fixed here - simply switch a node version...\n\nComments in this issue helps a lot, I wouldn't think of the node version issue at all, neither do anyone else quickly enough under the same circumstance.\n--",
